    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d721">

                <pb facs="1066" n="966" sameAs="frus:frus1934v01/pg966"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.4215 Air Pollution/582</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Canadian Secretary of State for External
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bennett</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the American Minister in Canada</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Robbins</hi>)<anchor n="58" xml:id="d721-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ottawa</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-12-31">December 31,
                        1934</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honour to refer to recent
                    discussions regarding claims against the Trail Smelter and to state that the
                    Canadian Government has been giving careful consideration to the possibility of
                    a solution on the lines discussed between officers of our government and
                    representatives of the Department of State and yourself at Washington.</p>
                <p>In the hope of reaching a speedy settlement of this issue, the Canadian
                    Government is prepared to accept the draft as revised on that occasion with the
                    following verbal changes in Article III, which it is believed will clarify the
                    questions without changing the intent;</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Article III—Question 2</hi>
                </p>
                <p>The first clause to read “In the event of the answer to the first part of the
                    preceding question being in the affirmative, etc”.</p>
                <pb facs="1067" n="967" xml:id="pg967"/>
                <p rendition="#flush">
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Question 3</hi>
                </p>
                <p>All words after “maintained” to be struck out and the words “by the Trail
                    Smelter” to be substituted.</p>
                <p>I enclose two copies of the draft which was under discussion in Washington,
                    amended to include the two changes indicated above.</p>
                <p>I should be glad to learn whether the Government of the United States are
                    prepared to enter into an agreement on this basis.<anchor n="59"
                        xml:id="d721-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">R. B. Bennett</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d721-f1" n="58" type="source" xml:id="d721-f1-content">
                <p>Copy transmitted to the Department by the Minister in Canada in his despatch No.
                    989, January 1, 1935; received January 3, 1935.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d721-f2" n="59" xml:id="d721-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram No. 20, March 20, 1935, 6 p.m. the Minister in Canada was authorized
                    “to sign convention conforming to Canadian draft that accompanied your
                    despatch.” The Canadian draft (not printed) was, with purely verbal changes in
                    the preamble, identical with the treaty signed at Ottawa, April 15, 1935: Treaty
                    Series No. 893, or 49 Stat. 3245.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
